---
.title = "Noise: sealed channels on TCP and the relay",
.description = "noise-tcp and noise-relay seal CapTP over TCP and the relay with Noise_XK, so the dialed key is proved and the path carries ciphertext.",
.date = .date("2026-08-11T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

`noise-tcp` seals a CapTP session between two glowcap peers over ordinary TCP.
Before any CapTP byte moves, the peers run a
[Noise_XK](https://noiseprotocol.org/noise.html) handshake with each other.
After that they speak only sealed records. Completing the handshake proves the
far end holds the dialed durable key. A captured handshake replays as a
refusal.

`keyed-tcp` uses the same designator but leaves CapTP frames in the clear.
[ADR-0107](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0107-sealed-byte-channel.md)
covers the channel.

[keyed-tcp is cleartext. noise-tcp seals the same sockets. noise-relay seals the CapTP stream so a relay only forwards ciphertext.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-noise-seal.svg'))

Upstream [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) ships no Noise
netlayer, so these symbols are glowcap to glowcap. To meet Goblins, use
[onion]($link.page('transports/onion')), [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))
with this repository's Goblins adapter, or cleartext `tcp` for testing. Prefer
`noise-tcp` when two glowcap peers already share a TCP route and only need the
stream sealed. Prefer iroh when those peers also need discovery or NAT
traversal.
[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) has the full decision
table.

