# Transport details

The sockets are the same ones `keyed-tcp` uses. The designator is the peer's
durable public key, and the bound address rides in the `direct-addresses`
hint:

```
ocapn://<64 hex chars>.noise-tcp?direct-addresses=127.0.0.1%3A9099
```

A listener binds a durable key. The advertised locator is what partners dial.
In Zig the profile is `glowcap.net.tcp.noise`. `driver.connect` and
`driver.listen` open sealed sessions over real sockets. The C driver loop and
all four native bindings dial and listen through the TCP provider package,
which owns `noise-tcp`, `keyed-tcp`, and testing `tcp` together.

`BlockingSession` borrows an already-open byte stream and cannot apply the
sealed-channel protocol, so its constructors reject `noise-tcp`. Use
`driver.Session` or the C driver loop for Noise connections.

# Through the relay

`noise-relay` is the same sealed channel over the
[relay]($link.page('relay')) pipe that `keyed-relay` uses. The CapTP peers
still handshake with each other, and the relay only moves opaque records.

Every native binding dials and registers through the relay provider package. A
browser peer speaks the same `noise-relay` through its managed WebSocket
connector. Rooms uses it for direct messages through the room's own relay,
beside the default WebRTC channel.
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) lists the checks.
